WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.309 --> 00:00:03.639
Have you ever wished there were a magical
way to easily remember foreign language words

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:05.600
and phrases, with little effort?

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.880
Nowadays, many language learners claim that
there is such a way, thanks to a flashcard

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.750
app called Anki.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:16.020
Anki claims to "make remembering things easy",
and help you study less, learn more, and do

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.770
all that a lot more efficiently than you would
with traditional memorization methods.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:24.930
Despite Anki's widespread popularity, many
people are shocked when I tell them that I

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:25.980
don't use Anki.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.910
In fact, I don't recommend it at all!

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.949
And in this video I am going to tell you why.

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:32.410
Hi, I'm Luca Lampariello.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.440
I'm a language coach who speaks 14 languages.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.520
On this channel, I help people learn foreign
languages faster, more efficiently, and in

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.020
a fun way.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.199
In this video, I'll share five reasons why
you shouldn't use Anki to learn foreign language

00:00:45.199 --> 00:00:46.410
words.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.440
Before we dive into the reasons why you should
not use ANKI, I want to first give you a little

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.969
more information about exactly what Anki is,
and why it claims to be so effective at helping

00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:58.809
you memorize words.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:04.809
The story starts (believe it or not) with
a 19th century German psychologist named Herman

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:05.840
Ebbinghaus.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.280
Ebbinghaus was fascinated by the human memory.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.670
In particular, he wanted to understand how
learning and forgetting play a role in the

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.040
memorization process.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.009
So, Ebbinghaus devised an experiment.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:21.540
He would attempt to learn a set of completely
new information, and then test his ability

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.280
to recall learned items over time.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.750
To make sure he couldn't rely on existing
knowledge, Ebbinghaus decided to learn "nonsense

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.890
syllables"—three-letter chunks of sound
that had no significance in his native German.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:40.540
He would make long lists of these syllables—for
example, "wid, zof, taz, bok, and lef", learn

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:45.719
them, and then document his journey towards
reciting each list perfectly from memory.

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:49.659
Crucially, Ebbinghaus tracked a number of
important variables in the process, including

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:56.299
number of repetitions, time between repetitions,
number of syllables, in the list, and so on.

00:01:56.299 --> 00:02:01.220
These experiments revealed to Ebbinghaus something
that we now call "the forgetting curve".

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:06.600
The curve is a graph that reveals the rate
at which information is forgotten over time.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.940
Through Ebbinghaus' work, we know that information
that is not reviewed is forgotten quickly,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:17.470
but information that is reviewed (or "repeated")
shortly before it is forgotten gets a "boost"

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.709
in your memory.

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:23.160
This reviewed memory will then be forgotten
at a slower rate than it was before.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.459
If additional reviews are completed, this
rate of forgetting will slow further and further

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:31.660
until we can consider the piece of information
effectively memorized.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:35.300
The forgetting curve reveals that the timing
of repetitions can be hugely important for

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.580
long-term memorization.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.650
It even leaves an enticing mystery: if we
knew exactly when we were about to forget

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:47.600
a piece of information, couldn't we then determine
when to review it, for maximum retention power?

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.530
That mystery is exactly why software programs
like Anki exist.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:57.610
These programs (called Spaced Repetition systems),
use data and algorithms to supposedly pinpoint

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.340
the ideal time to review a learned piece of
information.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:04.950
Following that logic, if you learn with such
software, you would then be memorizing information

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.560
in "the most efficient way", because you're
aligning your review sessions with your brain's

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.830
natural forgetting curve.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:16.050
Here's the thing: I have no gripes with Ebbinghaus,
or his forgetting curve.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:20.700
I'm actually a huge fan of the concept of
Spaced Repetition, and I use it all the time

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:21.700
in my learning.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.850
What I do not do, though, is use Spaced Repetition
software.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:29.319
I don't use Anki, Memrise, Supermemo, or anything
like it.

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:31.280
I never have.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.500
Actually to be fair, I have given ANKI a shot.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.159
I have toyed with it for a while to make this
exact video.

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:41.030
And now I know I will never use it for my
language learning.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.030
Why?

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:48.879
Well, I've got five reasons why, which are:
Making flashcards wastes learning time

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:57.400
Adding new cards can become an addiction
Reviewing old cards can become a chore

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:04.930
Flashcards take language out of context
Brain-friendly learning strategies make SRS

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:08.760
apps irrelevant
Let's dive right in:

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.209
Making Flashcards Wastes Learning Time
One of the hardest parts about learning a

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:15.790
language as an adult is finding the time to
get it all done.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:20.350
The demands of work and family life often
leave us precious few hours to devote to our

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.030
target languages.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:26.199
For that reason, I strive to spend most of
what language learning time I have actually

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:31.190
absorbing and using the language in a natural
way: by reading authentic texts, listening

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:35.450
to authentic podcasts, watching authentic
films, and having authentic conversations

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:36.669
with natives.

00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:41.050
If I were to inject Anki into my language
practice, I'd have to devote a chunk of that

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:45.920
time to inauthentic tasks, most of which boil
down to tedious data entry.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.139
To create a good-quality Anki card, you need
to:

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:56.810
Type in what goes on the front of the card
Type in what goes on the back of the card

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:04.810
Add an image file (if you can find one)
Add an audio file (if you can find one)

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:10.670
Decide what other information you want to
include, like tags, formatting, and different

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.500
card formats.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.220
Save the card
That's at least several minutes of work, and

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.720
only just for one card!

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:23.919
If you're creating a deck of tens, or hundreds,
or even thousands of cards (as some people

00:05:23.919 --> 00:05:27.120
do), that's A LOT of time lost!

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.340
Even if Anki does help speed up your memorization
process on the other side, that's a huge amount

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.980
of time spent up front just creating and managing
your flashcards.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.440
And that's if everything goes well!

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.260
From what I've been told, Anki isn't the simplest
and most functional piece of software, so

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:44.819
you'll probably lose even more time just figuring
out how to get everything working the way

00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:46.370
you want!

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:48.560
Not at all worth it, in my opinion.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.720
Save that time, and apply it directly to using
and absorbing your target language.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.100
Adding New Cards Can Become an Addiction
Despite the work that it takes to put together

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:02.289
a good deck of flashcards using Anki, a lot
of that setup is front-loaded.

00:06:02.289 --> 00:06:06.539
Once you've planned the structure of the deck,
configured the layout of individual flashcards,

00:06:06.539 --> 00:06:10.860
and then added enough cards to get started,
growing the deck actually becomes pretty easy.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.630
Assuming you know where all of your card data
is coming from, adding a new card can take

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:17.780
anywhere from a couple seconds to a minute.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:22.569
And while the ability to quickly add new cards
might seem like a good thing (and often is),

00:06:22.569 --> 00:06:26.000
it's something that can quickly become addicting.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.330
The whole value proposition of Anki is that
it can help you remember anything you want.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:35.350
Since Anki flashcards are entirely digital,
this actually seems feasible—nowadays, modern

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:40.500
smartphones make it trivial to carry card
decks containing thousands of cards or more,

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:41.560
anywhere you go.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:46.340
Essentially, Anki removes nearly all costs
associated with adding new flashcards to your

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:47.440
card deck.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.720
Assuming you have the time to make the additions,
there's nothing else preventing you from adding

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:56.500
every phrase you hear in your target language
right to your virtual "memory bank".

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.099
There's even software out there that can make
this simple process even easier.

00:07:01.099 --> 00:07:06.629
Using programs like subs2srs and voracious,
you can turn an entire movie or television

00:07:06.629 --> 00:07:12.020
show into an Anki deck of thousands of cards,
in just a few clicks of a button.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.140
You might wonder what I'm complaining about
here.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:18.241
Certainly having a fast way to turn movies
and TV shows into learning material is a good

00:07:18.241 --> 00:07:19.280
thing, right?

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.039
Yes, of course it is!

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:26.080
But if you don't know how to do all this in
moderation, the size of your Anki decks can

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:28.490
quickly spiral out of control.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:32.030
This is because once you've got a deck of
Anki cards, you need to actually learn them

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:33.080
and review them.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:39.860
This, incidentally, brings me to my next point:
Reviewing Old Cards Can Become a Chore

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.289
If you recall my explanation of the Forgetting
Curve, you'll remember that each time you

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:48.099
learn or review something, there's an ideal
point at which you should review it again.

00:07:48.099 --> 00:07:52.720
This helps you strengthen the memory and slow
the speed at which you'll forget it in the

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:53.720
future.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:58.129
As a software program, Anki's job is to show
you a piece of information (a flashcard),

00:07:58.129 --> 00:08:03.020
and then algorithmically determine when that
next ideal review should occur.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:07.690
So, for example, it might show you a card
for the first time, and test you on its contents.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:12.509
Based on that result, it could then decide
that you need to review again in a few hours,

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:14.430
or a few days.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:18.180
When it comes time for you to actually do
that review, Anki will show the card to you

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.510
automatically; you don't need to do any extra
work.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.229
This is fine for one card.

00:08:23.229 --> 00:08:27.440
But as you go through and learn dozens or
hundreds of cards, these reviews will start

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.889
to pile up.

00:08:28.889 --> 00:08:32.570
Before you know it, you could be spending
more than an hour each day going through your

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:37.880
Anki reviews—and that's before you even
get to learning new cards for that day.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.839
And if that sounds bad, then you shouldn't
even think about taking a day off.

00:08:41.839 --> 00:08:46.830
Because while you're taking your break (because
you're busy, sick, or just unwilling to review

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:50.060
that day), your Anki reviews are still piling
up.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:54.610
When you come back to Anki (assuming you will,
which is not guaranteed), you'll have a mountain

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.730
of reviews to work through.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.080
And that's terrible for motivation.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.779
People first become addicted to adding new
cards, but then become opposed to actually

00:09:02.779 --> 00:09:04.380
reviewing them.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:08.880
This becomes a vicious cycle which ultimately
causes learners to feel overwhelmed and give

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.570
up their language learning.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:14.670
I've seen it time and time again, and it's
not good for anyone.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:18.910
The tools you use should not only motivate
you to learn, but help you stay on the learning

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:21.320
path for as long as possible.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.380
Anki doesn't seem to do that for the vast
majority of people, which is a major reason

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.950
why I don't recommend it.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:29.120
But I'm not done yet.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.110
Let's move on to the next reason.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:36.310
Flashcards Take Language Out of Context
My next gripe against Anki is one that I have

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:40.829
against flashcards in general: by definition,
they remove the language you are learning

00:09:40.829 --> 00:09:43.190
from its natural context.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.279
Just think about it.

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:47.450
When you picture a flashcard in your head,
what do you see?

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.650
A simple card with a word or phrase in your
target language on one side, and the equivalent

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:55.670
expression in your native language on the
back.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:00.860
For all its bells and whistles, Anki boils
down to just that: a way to take foreign language

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:06.410
content and chop it up into small, isolated
pieces, so that each piece can be absorbed,

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:08.440
reviewed, and tested individually.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.920
Breaking down a lot of information into small
chunks is a great way to learn in general,

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:15.200
but it goes against how language inherently
works.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.610
Language is not just a series of isolated
words and phrases placed neatly next to each

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:22.510
other in a row, like so many beads on a string.

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:25.610
Rather, language functions as a network.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:31.250
Each word in a phrase, each phrase in a sentence,
and each sentence in a paragraph or utterance

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:34.350
serves to reinforce everything else around
it.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:38.620
If you remove any one of those things from
its network—the natural context that it

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.760
finds itself within—it begins to lose meaning.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.079
In fact, the loss of meaning can become so
great that in many cases, when you remove

00:10:46.079 --> 00:10:50.600
a word from its surrounding context, it becomes
essentially meaningless.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:54.680
You see this practically anytime you look
up a word in a dictionary.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.250
Most words you know have more than one meaning.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:01.600
But to determine which meaning is actually
being used in a given situation, you have

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:03.650
to look at the surrounding context.

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.560
To give a quick example, think of the word
"light".

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.000
On its own, light could mean the visible radiation
coming from the sun, or the relative weight

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.230
of an object.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:16.660
But if you put just "light" on one side of
a flashcard, you'd have no idea which of those

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:20.920
two kinds of "light" was intended—at least,
not without more context.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:24.839
However, if you had never heard the word "light"
before, and I showed you a video about the

00:11:24.839 --> 00:11:29.030
sun, you'd quickly learn one meaning of "light",
and automatically connect it to dozens of

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:35.060
words and phrases which can give you context
for that meaning, like "sun", "star", "solar

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.810
system", "radiation", "wavelength" and more.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:42.250
There are ways to mitigate the loss of context
that comes with taking target language content

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:47.570
and putting it on flashcards; however, that
generally requires you to squeeze more information

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:52.911
onto each flashcard, which can then make the
whole flashcard creation more laborious, as

00:11:52.911 --> 00:11:55.240
I explored in my first point.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:59.100
Given the issues that come along with creating
and managing flashcards with Anki, wouldn't

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:04.510
it be great if we could get all of the benefits
of spaced repetition, but without the extra

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:05.510
hassle?

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:06.510
We can!

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.660
Which is why, in my next point, I can say
that...

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:12.110
Brain-Friendly Learning Strategies Make SRS
Apps Irrelevant

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:15.630
You might remember that earlier, I mentioned
that I don't use Anki at all.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.160
You may also remember that after introducing
the concept of Spaced Repetition, I mentioned

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:23.149
that I'm a huge fan of the concept, and that
I use it all the time to learn languages.

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:24.149
So what gives?

00:12:24.149 --> 00:12:27.860
How is it possible to gain all the benefits
of Spaced Repetition, without also using a

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:31.920
program like Anki (and all the drawbacks that
come with it)?

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.860
It's surprisingly not all that difficult.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:38.380
Thinking back to Ebbinghaus' work on spaced
repetition and the forgetting curve, the main

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:42.769
idea revolves around the benefits of getting
repeated exposure to a piece of information

00:12:42.769 --> 00:12:44.550
over a long period of time.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:49.170
Though software like Anki optimizes exactly
when you do your review, there's not much

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:53.269
benefit lost from just making sure you spread
out your repetitions of a single piece of

00:12:53.269 --> 00:12:56.790
learning content over the course of a week,
or even a month.

00:12:56.790 --> 00:13:00.820
By reviewing regularly, and giving yourself
time to forget between reviews, you can leverage

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:05.290
the power of spaced repetition, without any
of the maintenance that comes with creating

00:13:05.290 --> 00:13:10.839
an Anki deck, and without getting sucked into
the endless cycle of adding cards and declining

00:13:10.839 --> 00:13:11.880
to review them.

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:15.610
This is exactly how I structure my Bidirectional
Translation method, which is the method I

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:18.190
follow to learn new languages every year.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:22.800
I take a short piece of content, learn it
deeply, and then review it in a variety of

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:26.402
ways over the course of a single week.

00:13:26.402 --> 00:13:27.402
Then, after that week, I trust that additional
repetitions will just come through learning

00:13:27.402 --> 00:13:28.402
and using the language.

00:13:28.402 --> 00:13:32.230
Here’s a short breakdown of how it works:
On day 1, I read the content and listen to

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:37.070
the audio, using the translation in my mother
tongue to try to understand the text as deeply

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:38.529
as possible.

00:13:38.529 --> 00:13:43.100
On day 2, I analyze the phonetic patterns
of the text—the pronunciation, intonation,

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.839
and word stress.

00:13:45.839 --> 00:13:51.380
On day 3, I review the content in a brand-new
way—usually through just listening to the

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:55.100
audio, just reading the text, or something
else entirely!

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:59.350
On day 4, I take the target language version
of the text and make a personalized translation

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:02.130
of it, in my native language.

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:06.509
On day 5, I take my native language translation
from the previous day, and try to verbally

00:14:06.509 --> 00:14:09.149
translate it back into my target language.

00:14:09.149 --> 00:14:14.110
And on day 6, I complete the cycle, by taking
my native-language translation and re-translating

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:17.500
it IN WRITING back into the original language.

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:22.230
Then, I make note of any errors, forgotten
words, or mistranslations.

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:25.880
This cycle of taking a piece of language content
and “processing it” in various ways is

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:31.110
incredibly powerful, and a lot more fun than
sitting in front of my computer, creating

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:33.630
digital flashcards every day!

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:37.130
And it's something you should definitely try,
if you get a chance!

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:42.230
Alright, those are all of the reasons why
I don't use Anki, and why I believe you shouldn't

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:43.230
either!

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:47.250
There are much better ways, which I'll explore
in even more detail in a future video.

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:49.399
For now, let's recap the five reasons.

00:14:49.399 --> 00:14:54.820
I believe you shouldn't use Anki to learn
vocabulary in a foreign language because:

00:14:54.820 --> 00:15:01.589
Making flashcards wastes learning time
Adding new cards can become an addiction

00:15:01.589 --> 00:15:11.050
Reviewing old cards can become a chore
Flashcards take language out of context

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:15.120
Brain-friendly learning strategies make SRS
apps irrelevant

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:18.949
If you want to take a deeper dive into my
Anki-free strategies for Spaced Repetition,

00:15:18.949 --> 00:15:23.470
definitely check out the courses in my Become
a Master Language Learner series, both of

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:26.370
which use these strategies to full effect!

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:31.269
I've got a beginner course about my aforementioned
Bidirectional Translation Method, and an intermediate

00:15:31.269 --> 00:15:33.980
course about overcoming the Intermediate Plateau!

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:38.090
As usual I left a link in the description
box and in the comment pinned on top in the

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:39.680
comment section.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:40.680
That's all for now!

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.130
Thanks for watching, and I'll see you soon.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:43.620
Happy language learning!

